this nation is called the United States  of America and doesn't like many other  countries its name was deliberately  chosen France for example comes from the  Latin word Frankie a land of the Franks  but it wasn't chosen by a committee of  Frenchmen it simply stuck over the  centuries the question is who named the  United States why was that name selected  and what were the alternatives so prior  to the American Revolution these lands  were known to the British as British  North America or simply the colonies and  the officials that lived there often  used the name the United colonies of  North America the name America in case  you're wondering had been a commonly  used term for a couple of centuries by  this point and was derived from America  Vespucci an early Explorer this along  with the rare a Columbia named for  Christopher Columbus was used to  describe the entire land mass there were  not just the British colonies there when  tension started to rise people began to  use different terms to describe the 13  colonies when the American Revolution  broke out it was Thomas Jefferson who  first used the term United States of  America and its use was formalized by  John Hancock and the Continental  Congress in late 1776 colloquially  United States in American states were  frequently used by citizens and it's one  of the reasons why the name took hold  this didn't mean that the name was fixed  though when dealing with the French  Benjamin Franklin used the term United  States of North America and when peace  was made with the British they did so  under the name the United States so that  settled them right well no there were  some in the new USA that didn't like the  name and wish to change it to something  less descriptive and more poetic  the number one alternative proposed was  Columbia and whilst this was the most  popular alternative many was simply used  to calling the country the United States  by now and so felt that there was no  need to change anything by 